[Music]  russia's massive division of his  democratic neighbor ukraine has drawn  sharp condemnation from all over the  world here in new york ukraine's u.n  ambassador turned directly to russia's  ambassador last night and said this  quote there is no purgatory for war  criminals they go straight to hell join  us now with some perspective is ian  bremmer the president and founder of  your razor group and g zero media good  morning and how you doing to you good to  see you guys so you called this a moment  um a turning point and global order you  said there will be thousands of innocent  civilian lives that will be at cost um  where do you see this going um this is  uh now a cold war  between the united states and all of its  allies very unified on this issue uh and  russia and that means uh we've we're  cutting off diplomatic engagement uh  we're cutting off economic engagement uh  you know for the last 30 years no one  was living through a cuban missile  crisis moment no one thought that you  had the potential for military  confrontation in europe anymore that is  gone today you have 200 000 russian  troops that have been built up and  arrayed all along ukrainian support  ukraine's borders and they are now  invading and they are  going to be massive does this have to be  another cold war it's an elementary  question but i think it's probably in  the minds of many americans as they wake  up this morning do we have to contest  this taking of ukraine or potential  taking of ukraine i think we do  because i think that we worked uh over  the course of the last several months to  tell the russians very clearly and in a  united fashion  that an invasion of ukraine while we  would not defend them directly  we consider them a legitimate democracy  we believe in their self-determination  that means that we have already sent  them uh a significant amount of war  materiel that they are using to defend  themselves we have sent troops to the  front lines in the baltic states uh in  poland in romania in bulgaria  nato is now going to have functionally  a border  right up against a hostile  russia-controlled ukraine and there'll  be partisan fighting on the ground in  ukraine i think it's incredibly hard  given putin's decision to put this genie  back in the bottle  putin says that he's not intending to  occupy ukraine he just wants to  demilitarize the country what does that  even mean it means he's lying um putin  has told us for the last two months he's  told biden he's told macron he's told  schultz he's told johnson that he has no  intention of invading ukraine he's lied  in consistent and staggering fashion and  so tragically for the ukrainian people  they're now saying they have no  intention to occupy ukraine but russia  has already said that they consider that  the ukrainian nation is a part of russia  it is very clear that they intend to  overthrow this ukrainian government and  they intend to integrate it in some way  within a broader russian structure if if  another country interferes putin warned  consequences greater than any have seen  in history any of you have seen in  history is that an overt nuclear threat  i guess it is but it's cheap because the  united states and all the nato allies  have already made very clear to  president putin that they will not put  troops on the ground to defend ukraine  itself it's not a member of nato it has  no membership action plan what putin is  saying is that this country is mine i am  taking it i am working to reintegrate  russian empire keep in mind that putin  has said on the record many times that  he considers the dissolution of the  soviet union to be the greatest  geopolitical tragedy of the 20th century  and he believes that it is his legacy to  reverse that that is the message that he  is presently sending and he's willing to  spill russian blood never mind ukrainian  blood  we've already seen planes get shot down  over the last 24 hours i mean there's  shut down right absolutely i mean this  is putin saying i am going to make it my  legacy by enemies necessary by all means  that's right now you've also said that  they have turned into an international  pariah a parade that occupies a lot of  the economic space globally and now how  do you go about crippling russia's  economical infrastructure without taking  down the international  economic balance well two points first  it's going to be very costly  particularly for the europeans the  united states have very little  dependence economically on russia but  the europeans in terms of gas in terms  of trade and agriculture in terms of you  know investment from oligarchs that's  going away so you're going to have  significant spike in gas costs for the  europeans you'll have shortages this  will probably have an impact of i'd say  you know one point of gdp on much of  europe minimum over the course of the  year but beyond what you do to russia  there's also the question of how does  this affect china right because the  americans are putting sanctions on  russia and the chinese aren't going to  want to break those sanctions which why  the chinese have been critical of  increasing sanctions final point is  sanctions can only do so much putin  knows what's coming he knew it was  coming yesterday he knows what's coming  today the maximum level of u.s and  european sanctions that will go on  russia and that the biggest sanctions  that will ever have been imposed on a  major power post-soviet collapse will be  less than what we've seen or roughly  about what we've seen on iran so if  sanctions can only do so much how far  should the u.s go i i think you still  have to go there  you still go there but is that what does  go there mean though well go there means  that nordstrom 2 the pipeline with  germany's cost 11 billion dollars to  build is dead it will not be used by the  russians  let's cut to the chase here i mean  legally we have no obligation right now  to protect ukraine we don't have to be  involved at all that's right but we have  nato partners that are on the border of  uh poland poland borders ukraine uh and  then they're the baltic states former  soviet states that's right in your view  putin wants those back so do you think  this war is coming to nato um that's a  hot war i i don't believe that but i  believe that putin will sense weakness  and division and he'll play on it and  one of the reasons why you have to go  there in maximal fashion with the  sanctions is because you're protecting  the baltic states you're protecting  poland if the americans were now to say  ah we need to be able to do business  with the russians the europeans say the  same thing you could break nato i mean  i've had those conversations directly  with the leadership of nato in the last  several days i was just the munich  security conference like there's the  nato is incredibly unified right now  that is the one silver lining that you  can take away from all this there are  very few okay ann bremmer thank you so  much for joining us

